    <view x="10" wrappingAllowedX="true" y="65" frameRate="30.0" minPycor="-16" height="433" showTickCounter="true" patchSize="13.0" fontSize="10" wrappingAllowedY="true" width="433" tickCounterLabel="ticks" maxPycor="16" updateMode="1" maxPxcor="16" minPxcor="-16"></view>
    <button x="10" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="95" display="setup">setup</button>
    <button x="115" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="95" display="go">go</button>
    <button x="220" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="95" display="go once">go</button>
    <monitor x="455" precision="3" y="65" height="60" fontSize="12" width="155" display="Mean score">mean [score] of turtles</monitor>
    <plot x="455" autoPlotX="true" yMax="100.0" autoPlotY="true" yAxis="Number of agents" y="140" xMin="-5.0" height="350" legend="false" xMax="6.0" yMin="0.0" width="440" xAxis="Score" display="Score histogram">
      <setup></setup>
      <update>set-plot-x-range (min [score] of turtles - 0.5) (max [score] of turtles + 0.5)
set-histogram-num-bars (max [score] of turtles - min [score] of turtles + 1)
histogram [score] of turtles</update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="1" display="agents" color="-13345367" legend="false">
        <setup></setup>
        <update></update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## O_0: Stationary random rock-paper-scissors

### Purpose

Show that agents can identify neighboring agents and play a game with them.

### How to use

Click **setup**, then **go** to run continuously or **go once** for a single tick. The run stops at 2000 ticks. This fixed limit is written directly in `go`, without a slider.

### Setup and arena

`setup` clears the world, colors patches white, calls `populate`, and initializes the clock with `reset-ticks`. `populate` selects 100 distinct patches and creates one agent on each, calling `initialize-agent`.

The arena is a 33 by 33 wrapping grid. Each patch holds at most one agent. Agents remain stationary, with no birth or death.

### Agent states

`turtles-own` gives each agent its own states:

- `score`: accumulated RPS payoff, initially 0. It persists across ticks.
- `played?`: whether the agent played at least once in the current tick, initially false. It records participation rather than limiting games.
- `original-color`: the color restored when clearing participation, set to blue in O_0.

### The go procedure

1. Check `if ticks >= 2000 [ stop ]` before any further games.
2. Call `clear-participation` to restore white patches and original agent colors, and set `played?` to false. Scores and positions remain unchanged.
3. Select `players`: agents with at least one agent on their `neighbors4` patches.
4. Execute `ask players [ play ]`. Every selected agent completes its procedure before `tick`.
5. Advance CLOCK with `tick`, which also updates the score plot.

Isolated agents stay in the population but do not execute `play`. They have no games and remain at score zero. Because occupancy is fixed, they remain isolated until setup creates a new arrangement.

### Finding partners and playing

`neighbors4` selects the four cardinally adjacent patches, including across wrapping boundaries. Diagonal patches and the agent's own patch are excluded. `turtles-on neighbors4` identifies agents occupying those patches.

Within `play`, `focal` stores the initiating agent. Its `opponents` are neighboring agents with a higher `who` number. `ask opponents` visits all those neighbors in random order. It does not choose a single partner with `one-of`.

Inside `ask opponents`, the active agent is the neighbor. Both random moves are generated there. The neighbor receives `payoff` and `focal` receives its negative. Every game therefore updates both players.

The higher-`who` comparison avoids duplicate games: each occupied neighboring pair plays exactly once per tick. Agents can also play with lower-numbered neighbors when those neighbors initiate the game. An agent can play up to four games per tick. A selected agent with no higher-numbered opponents initiates no games, but participates through its lower-numbered neighbors.

This encounter structure prepares O_0 for the later Hammond–Axelrod sequence: agents interact with all occupied cardinal neighbors, while the game and other mechanisms can change.

### Game rules and supporting procedures

Each player independently chooses rock (0), paper (1), or scissors (2), with equal probability for every game.

`rps-payoff` reports 0 for identical moves, +1 for rock against scissors, paper against rock, or scissors against paper, and -1 otherwise. No memory, learning, or persistent choice is used.

`receive-payoff` adds the points to score and calls `mark-participation`. The winner gains 1, the loser loses 1, and ties change neither score. The total and mean population score stay zero.

### Participation display

`mark-participation` turns the player red, sets `played?` to true, and turns its patch yellow. Both players are marked, including in a tie. `played?` does not prevent games with other neighbors.

The same occupied neighboring pairs play every tick, so the highlights reappear in the same locations. RPS choices and activation order are random; partner availability is fixed.

### Histogram and mean score

The histogram shows current cumulative scores, with one bin per integer. The monitor reports mean score. At this sparse density, isolated agents can create a persistent peak at zero. A bell shape is not guaranteed for the full population.

### Experimental duration

A tick includes all scheduled pair interactions, not one game. With the fixed limit, the model completes 2000 ticks and subsequent calls to go perform no further games. Click setup for a fresh run.

Hammond and Axelrod use 2000 periods in their standard experiments. Sharing this horizon does not make O_0 their ethnocentrism model: O_0 is the preliminary RPS interaction model.

Source: Hammond and Axelrod (2006), The Evolution of Ethnocentrism, manuscript pp. 8–9: https://websites.umich.edu/~axe/research/Hammond%20and%20Axelrod%20JCR%2006.pdf

### Credits

Professor Jose Manuel MAGALLANES  
Complexity and ABM, 2026  
DACSS - UMASS
]]></info>
